From 6799aa46c25023cda6e923dd4b6663f1542efc26 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Rockefeller <alanrockefeller@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 20:11:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/12] SH-aLRT: draw the replicate pattern frequencies once
 per traversal

testOneBranch() re-seeds every OpenMP thread's random stream with
ran_seed + thread number on every call, and the replicate loop is
statically scheduled, so each of the n-3 internal branches draws exactly
the same `times` bootstrap pattern-frequency vectors -- nsite random
numbers per replicate, regenerated for every branch. On a 130-taxon ITS
alignment that regeneration (createBootstrapAlignment, random_resampling,
get_rn_dbl) was ~20% of a whole `-n 5 --alrt 1000` run.

Draw the vectors once per testAllBranches() traversal, while scoring the
first branch, and replay them for the others. The cache is keyed on the
replicate count, pattern count, alignment and OpenMP team size (a
different team size changes which stream draws which replicate, so it is
re-recorded), is reset at the start of each traversal and freed at its
end, and is skipped above 128 MB. The RELL sums are factored into one
helper used by both paths, so each replicate's frequencies enter the
same sums in the same order: supports are bit-identical. The serial
(non-OpenMP) build keeps advancing the global stream between branches
and is unchanged.

SPRNG streams depend only on (stream number, seed, parameter); skipping
init_sprng on replayed calls only leaves its global stream counter
lower, which nothing reads for values.

Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QBkV69MxCAg5qwKtaWSJ2d
