From d5cef74e84f50d535f5a2d8e09536ab77734aaee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Rockefeller <alanrockefeller@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 20:35:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 05/12] UFBoot: print sorted topology strings without
 per-subtree streams

With WT_SORT_TAXA, MTree::printTree() prints every subtree into its own
ostringstream, collects them in a heap-allocated set and, with -bnni,
formats each branch length through iostreams (glibc printf with
multiprecision digits). The UFBoot candidate and refined boot trees are
printed this way thousands of times; after the previous changes it was
still ~8% of an -m MFP -B 1000 -bnni run.

Add MTree::printSortedTreeString(), which appends the same text to a
std::string: subtrees are built as strings and ordered by smallest taxon
ID (keeping the first of equal IDs, as the set does), taxon IDs use
std::to_string, and branch lengths use std::to_chars with the
precision and notation the stream would have used -- fixed for a leaf,
whose stream printTree() switches to fixed, the default notation for an
internal node. std::to_chars with a precision is specified to match
printf's %.*f / %.*g, which is what the stream produces; non-finite
values and anything to_chars cannot fit fall back to a stream. Checked
against ostream on 10.6M values (random bit patterns, branch-length
magnitudes, decimal rounding ties, signed zero) at precisions 1-17 in
both notations: no differences.

It only handles WT_SORT_TAXA with WT_TAXON_ID, WT_BR_LEN, WT_BR_LEN_SHORT
and WT_NEWLINE, and returns false otherwise; IQTreeMix,
PhyloSuperTreeUnlinked and PhyloTreeMixlen, which print their own way,
always return false, and the callers then use printTree() as before.

