Subject: [PATCH 08/12] Kernels: skip the OpenMP runtime when the tree has one
 thread

computeLikelihoodDervSIMD() enters a parallel region on every Newton
step of every branch, and computeTraversalInfo() on every traversal.
With -T 1 each of those is a serialized region (__kmp_fork_call,
__kmp_serialized_parallel, a dynamic-schedule dispatch per packet):
~5% of an -m MFP -B 1000 -bnni run.

When num_threads is 1, run the same loops directly. The Derv packet
body becomes a lambda taking the reduction variables as parameters, so
inside the OpenMP loop they still bind to the threads' reduction
copies. A one-thread team runs the packets in order into copies that
start at +0.0 -- a sum started at +0.0 can never end at -0.0 under
round-to-nearest -- and then adds them to variables that are still
+0.0, which leaves them unchanged; summing directly gives the same
values. The serialized team's thread number is 0, so the traversal's
scratch buffer is the same too. Multi-threaded runs are unchanged.

